  "query_preview": "..\n(This evening through next Friday)\nIssued at 1226 PM CDT Sat Aug 29 2026\n\nSurface high pressure over the Mid-Atlantic continues to feed dry\nair into the Mid-South. As a result, despite a strengthening\nupper ridge and temperatures rising into the lower to mid 90s, at\nleast the humidity is in check today with dewpoints in the 60s.\nHumidity will start to return tomorrow as the surface high pushes\neast and southerly flow develops. The higher dewpoints will be\nmost apparent along the Tennessee River and Alabama/Mississippi \nborder on Sunday, while temperatures climb to the mid and upper \n90s due to the strengthening upper ridge.\n\nThe upper ridge will be parked over the region for all of next\nweek. As humidity gradually increases due to persistent southerly\nflow, heat index values are likely to reach advisory levels\n(105\u00b0 or greater). There is a low chance this could happen as \nearly as Monday over parts of the area, though it is more likely \ntoward midweek. The ridge will stifle precipitation chances for \nthe most part. A weak disturbance moving westward across the Deep \nSouth may weaken the ridge a bit by the end of next week, \nresulting in isolated showers and thunderstorms. We are not seeing\nany significant systems or rainfall through next weekend, with \nabove normal temperatures and below normal rainfall continuing for\nthe foreseeable future.",
